was not reliable enough.

To solve this, I worked on a hybrid approach consisting of:

Step 1: Annotation-based targeting

Use the Veeva annotation to determine which claim-related text needs to be located.

Step 2: Locate relevant document content

Extract and inspect Word paragraphs to identify the content corresponding to the annotation.

Step 3: Contextual/LLM processing

Use LLM reasoning where needed to understand the difference between:

original approved claim,
text appearing in the Word asset, and
updated approved claim.
Step 4: Generate the appropriate document wording

Rather than blindly replacing the entire paragraph with the new claim, generate wording that preserves the surrounding document context.

Step 5: Apply the update

Reuse centralized functionality from document_claim_updater.py for the actual document modification.

Step 6: Preserve formatting

The replacement logic was designed with special attention to retaining existing Word formatting rather than reconstructing entire paragraphs and unintentionally changing their appearance.

This separation between identifying what the content should become and performing the physical document edit made the implementation more maintainable.

7. Extending Auto Update to PPTX Documents

After implementing the DOCX workflow, I began extending the same architecture to PowerPoint presentations.

PPTX introduces an additional challenge because text can appear across:

slides,
text boxes,
shapes, and
multiple text runs.

A presentation may also contain multiple annotations associated with several different claims.

The implementation therefore focuses on processing only the annotation associated with the claim currently being updated.

The workflow includes:

Receive the annotation text.
Locate the corresponding text inside the PPTX asset.
Confirm that the targeted claim content exists.
Retrieve the relevant PowerPoint text.
Compare its context against the previous claim.
Use the LLM to determine how the text should change based on the newly approved claim.
Pass the resulting text to the PowerPoint claim-update logic.
Preserve unrelated content and annotations.
Generate only the required updated document version.

The same centralized document_claim_updater.py logic is reused so that the Auto Update Agent remains responsible for orchestration while document-format-specific operations remain modular.

8. Document Processing and Format Preservation

Throughout the project, I worked with multiple document-processing approaches and libraries for:

DOCX
PPTX
PDF
HTML

One of the main practical lessons was that text replacement inside enterprise documents cannot be treated as plain string manipulation.

Word and PowerPoint frequently divide visible sentences into multiple runs containing different formatting properties.

Replacing an entire paragraph or shape can therefore result in:

Font changes
Font-size changes
Color loss
Bold/italic loss
Layout differences

The implementation evolved toward run-aware and format-preserving replacement wherever possible.

PDF processing introduced additional complications because rendered PDFs are fundamentally different from editable Office source formats, particularly when subset fonts are embedded.

This reinforced the architectural decision to modify the original DOCX/PPTX source assets whenever possible rather than treating the generated PDF rendition as the primary editable document.

9. Document Versioning and Data Integrity

A major focus throughout Auto Update Agent development was maintaining document and version integrity.

Special care was taken around scenarios where a document contains several annotations.

A naïve implementation could create one new document version for every processed annotation.

Instead, the workflow was designed so that multiple necessary modifications can be applied to the document while controlling when the resulting document is uploaded and versioned.

This helps prevent:

Duplicate document versions
Repeated uploads
Incorrect annotation-version relationships
Partial document modifications
Unexpected overwrites

Extensive logging was also incorporated throughout this workflow so that each update can be traced through stages such as:

